nk","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#configuration","content":"Each sink connector is configured in its own separate configuration file within the connectors directory specified in the main runtime config."},"1769":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-7","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#configuration","content":"Main runtime config (config.toml):"},"1770":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-8","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#configuration","content":"Sink connector config (connectors/stdout.toml):"},"1771":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-9","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#environment-variable-overrides","content":"Configuration properties can be overridden using environment variables. The pattern follows: IGGY_CONNECTORS_SINK_[KEY]_[PROPERTY]"},"1772":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-10","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#environment-variable-overrides","content":"For example, to override the enabled property for a sink with ID stdout:"},"1773":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-11","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"Let's implement the example sink connector, which will simply print the messages to the standard output."},"1774":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-12","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"Additionally, our sink connector will have its own state, which can be used e.g. to track the overall progress or store some relevant information when ingesting the data further to the external sources or tooling."},"1775":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-13","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"Also, when implementing the sink connector, make sure to use the sink_connector! macro to expose the FFI interface and allow the connector runtime to register the sink with the runtime."},"1776":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-14","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"And finally, each sink should have its own, custom configuration, which is passed along with the unique plugin ID via expected new() method."},"1777":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-15","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"Let's start by defining the internal state and the public sink connector along with its own configuration."},"1778":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-16","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"We can invoke the expected macro to expose the FFI interface and allow the connector runtime to register the sink within the runtime."},"1779":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-17","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"At a bare minimum, we need to add the following dependencies to the Cargo.toml file to compile the plugin at all:"},"1780":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-18","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"dashmap"},"1781":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-19","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"once_cell"},"1782":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-20","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"tracing"},"1783":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-21","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"Now, let's implement the Sink trait for our StdoutSink struct."},"1784":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-22","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"It's also important to note, that the supported format(s) might vary depending on the connector implementation. For example, you might expect JSON as the payload format, which can be then easily parsed and processed by upstream components such as data transforms, but at the same time, you could support the other formats and let the user decide which one to use."},"1785":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-23","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"For example, you can match against the payload enum field containing the deserialized value to process (or not) the consumed message(s)."},"1786":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-24","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"While the schema of messages (that will be consumed from the Iggy stream), cannot be controlled by the sink connector itself, the built-in configuration allows to decide what's the expected format of the messages (the particular StreamDecoder will be used)."},"1787":{"id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink-25","page_id":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/connectors/sinks/sink#sample-implementation","content":"Keep in mind, that it might be sometimes difficult/impossible e.g. to transform one format to another e.g. JSON to SBE or so, and in such a case, the consumed messages will be ignored."},"1788":