963,"We can invoke the expected macro to expose the FFI interface and allow the connector runtime to register the source within the runtime."],[1547,"We could make use of more advanced components such as ClientProvider, pass the custom configuration built via console args to choose between the different protocols as all the available clients implement the same Client trait and so on."],[1544,"We will begin with installing the Iggy client crate - execute cargo add iggy in your terminal. Next, install tokio.rs dependency with cargo add tokio as we will use the asynchronous runtime. Eventually, modify your main.rs, so it looks like this:"],[1533,"We will build a producer (publishing messages to a stream) and a consumer (reading those messages back). In a real system, a service can be both at the same time - publishing its own events while consuming events from other services."],[622,"We've also built the benchmarking platform where anyone can upload the benchmarks and compare the results with others. This is the another open-source project available here."],[1304,"Web UI"],[29,"Web UI - SvelteKit dashboard for stream/topic management, message browsing with JSON/string/XML decoders, user management, and real-time terminal."],[21,"Web UI dashboard (embedded or standalone), CLI with shell completions, Prometheus metrics, OpenTelemetry"],[580,"WebSocket"],[647,"WebSocket"],[751,"WebSocket"],[771,"WebSocket"],[807,"WebSocket"],[792,"WebSocket provides bidirectional streaming over HTTP upgrade. Iggy uses a custom compio-ws implementation that bridges the gap between tungstenite's poll-based model and compio's completion-based model."],[794,"WebSocket runs on all shards (not just shard 0) and supports optional TLS."],[692,"WebSocket uses a custom compio-ws implementation that bridges tungstenite with compio's completion-based I/O model. It runs on all shards and supports optional TLS."],[1147,"WebSocket: Optional TLS. Enable with websocket.tls.enabled = true."],[1,"Welcome"],[1524,"What we will build"],[1581,"What we've just achieved is merely the tip of an iceberg, however, it should give you a good understanding of what Iggy is all about, and hopefully, it wasn't too difficult to follow and get things up and running for the first time. Feel free to take a look at the more advanced examples, how to make use of IggyClient wrapper on top of the existing implementations of the Client trait, what happens when you start multiple producers and consumers and so on. Happy tweaking! :)"],[1521,"When a segment reaches its size limit (default 1 GiB), it is sealed and a new segment is created"],[1985,"When an empty string or plain address is passed to new_connection(), it defaults to TCP."],[1740,"When auto_create_table is enabled, the following table structure is created:"],[1911,"When cdc_backend is set to pg_replicate but the feature is not enabled, the connector will return a clear initialization error."],[1598,"When consumers join or leave a consumer group, the server triggers cooperative partition rebalancing. Partitions are redistributed among active members. During rebalancing, there is a pending revocation phase (configurable timeout, default 30s) to ensure in-progress message processing can complete before partitions move to new owners."],[811,"When enabled, the server expects periodic heartbeat messages from connected clients. Clients that miss heartbeats may be disconnected."],[349,"When implementing Sink, make sure to use the sink_connector! macro to expose the FFI interface and allow the connector runtime to register the sink with the runtime.\nEach sink should have its own, custom configuration, which is passed along with the unique plugin ID via expected new() method."],[598,"When polling messages, you can choose from several strategies:"],[707,"When running the container, make sure to include the additional capabilities required by io_uring and CPU affinity:"],[611,"When sending messages, you can control partition routing:"],[686,